---
title: 'Series Labels'
description: '$framework Charts support styling series data labels, controlling their position and orientation, and avoiding collisions between labels, markers and other series geometry.'
---

Series data labels display the value of a data point directly on the chart. These are configured on the `label` property of each series.

Please see the [API Reference](#api-reference) for the full list of available options, which vary slightly between series types.

## Styling

Enable labels with `label.enabled`, then style them with the following options.

{% chartExampleRunner title="Label Styling" name="label-showcase" type="generated" /%}

```js format="snippet"
{
    series: [
        {
            // ...
            label: {
                enabled: true,
            },
        },
    ],
}
```

In this example:

- The label text is styled with properties such as `color` and `fontWeight`. Other available options include `fontSize`, `fontStyle` and `fontFamily`.
- The label itself has a fill and border, configured with properties such as `fill` and `border`. Other available options include `fillOpacity`, `cornerRadius` and `padding`. See [Fills & Borders](./fills-borders/) for more details.
- The `insideStyle` and `outsideStyle` properties override these text and box styles for when the resolved label placement sits inside or outside the series node — used here to swap between a dark-on-light and light-on-dark treatment.
- Bar-family labels can additionally be rotated with `orientation`. See [Orientation](#orientation) for more details.
- Providing `placement` as an ordered array lets a label fall back to an alternative position. See [Placement](#placement) for more details.

## Placement

The available label positions are series-specific. These include `'inside-start'` or `'outside-end'` for a bar series, and `'top'` or `'left'` for a bubble series.

See the [API Reference](/options/) for the full list of placement values per series type.

Funnel and Pyramid Series place their labels with `'inside-center'`, `'inside-start'`, `'inside-end'`, `'outside-start'` and `'outside-end'` along the stage axis, and with `'inside-before'`, `'inside-after'`, `'outside-before'` and `'outside-after'` on the same sides as `stageLabel.placement`. A Pyramid Series centres its label on the stage and limits its width to the part of the stage the text sits across, so a long label wraps more in a narrow stage than in a wide one. An outside placement is rejected when it would leave the series area, in which case the label falls back to the next placement in the array; `aspectRatio` and `spacing` are the options that make room for one.

Cone Funnel Series combine the side of the divider with the position along it, giving nine values from `'start-before'` to `'end-after'`. `start` and `end` are the two sides of the divider and `middle` sits on it, while `before`, `center` and `after` run along it. The single-word values `'before'`, `'middle'` and `'after'` are deprecated aliases of `'start-center'`, `'middle-center'` and `'end-center'`.

A Cone Funnel label is always drawn clear of the divider line, so the series has no `insideStyle` or `outsideStyle` options.

{% chartExampleRunner title="Label Placement" name="label-position" type="generated" /%}

```js format="snippet"
{
    series: [
        {
            // ...
            label: {
                enabled: true,
                placement: ['top', 'bottom', 'left', 'right'],
                spacing: 6,
            },
        },
    ],
}
```

In this example:

- Providing `placement` as an ordered array allows the label to fallback to an alternative position if the first doesn't fit or collides with another item.
    - This is affected by [Orientation](#orientation) and other [Collision Avoidance](#collision-avoidance) options.
    - Resize the example to see the fallback placements in action.
- `spacing` sets the pixel distance between a label and its anchor and is ignored when the resolved placement is centred.

## Orientation

Bar-family series can rotate their labels using the `label.orientation` option. This accepts `'horizontal'`, `'vertical'` or `'vertical-reversed'`, or an ordered array of fallback orientations.

{% chartExampleRunner title="Label Orientation" name="label-orientation" type="generated" /%}

```js format="snippet"
{
    series: [
        {
            type: 'bar',
            // ...
            label: {
                enabled: true,
                orientation: ['horizontal', 'vertical'],
                wrapping: 'never',
            },
        },
    ],
}
```

In this example:

- Providing `orientation` as an ordered array allows the label to fallback to an alternative orientation if the first doesn't fit or collides with another item.
    - This is affected by [Placement](#placement) and other [Collision Avoidance](#collision-avoidance) options.
    - Resize the example to see the fallback placements in action.

## Collision Avoidance

As well as using [fallback placement](#placement) and [fallback orientation](#orientation) options, labels can also wrap, truncate or be hidden when they collide with other elements or don't fit within provided `maxWidth`/`maxHeight` values.

{% chartExampleRunner title="Label Fitting" name="label-fitting" type="generated" /%}

```js format="snippet"
{
    series: [
        {
            // ...
            label: {
                enabled: true,
                placement: 'inside-end',
                maxWidth: 70,
                maxHeight: 54,
                wrapping: 'on-space',
                truncate: true,
            },
        },
    ],
}
```

In this example:

- `maxWidth` and `maxHeight` specify the maximum label size.
- `wrapping` (`'on-space'`, `'always'`, `'hyphenate'`, `'never'`) controls how overflowing text wraps within the provided size or bar boundary.
- `truncate` truncates whatever still doesn't fit, appending an ellipsis.

Pie and Donut Series configure these options on `calloutLabel` and `sectorLabel` rather than on `label`. See [Pie Series Label Fitting](./pie-series/#label-fitting) for an example.

Series label collision avoidance is separate from [axis label collision avoidance](./axes-labels/#collision-avoidance), which is configured independently on each axis.

### Reducing the Font Size

Bar, histogram, waterfall, range bar, line, area, scatter, bubble, pie and donut labels can shrink to fit instead of wrapping, truncating or being hidden. Set `minimumFontSize` to the smallest size the label may use:

```js format="snippet"
{
    series: [
        {
            // ...
            label: {
                enabled: true,
                placement: 'inside-center',
                wrapping: 'on-space',
                minimumFontSize: 8,
            },
        },
    ],
}
```

The label picks the largest size between its `fontSize` and `minimumFontSize` at which the whole text fits. `wrapping` applies at each size tried, so a label can wrap and shrink together to fit. Only when the text still does not fit at `minimumFontSize` is it truncated or hidden, so shrinking is always preferred over an ellipsis.

Line, area, scatter and bubble labels are positioned around their data points, so they shrink to resolve collisions as well as to fit. Every placement is tried at the configured size first, and only when none is clear of the surrounding labels and markers is the label reduced to the largest size at which one is.

### Hiding Labels

When any of these strategies are used but fail to find a satisfactory resolution, the label is hidden by default.

Use `collision.alwaysShow: true` to force the label to remain visible, or `collision.alwaysShow: false` to allow labels to be hidden even when no other strategies are enabled.

{% chartExampleRunner title="Label Collision Threshold" name="label-threshold" type="generated" /%}

```js format="snippet"
{
    series: [
        {
            // ...
            label: {
                enabled: true,
                collision: {
                    alwaysShow: true,
                },
            },
        },
    ],
}
```

### Threshold

Collisions are defined as the edge of one label hitting the edge of another element.

Use a `collision.threshold` value to ensure labels are a minimum pixel distance from obstacles, or a negative value to allow labels to overlap somewhat.

```js format="snippet"
{
    series: [
        {
            // ...
            label: {
                enabled: true,
                collision: {
                    threshold: 4,
                },
            },
        },
    ],
}
```

